          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as true. The rest of her turn intensifies and elaborates that avowal by claiming that her love exceeds ordinary measures and even verbal expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The focal expression asserts the existence and exceptional degree of Goneril’s filial love, but it does not create, renew, or affirm a commitment to perform duties or remain bound to Lear. Her comparison with how a child loves a father invokes the family relationship only as a measure of affection, not as an undertaking. Lear’s subsequent grant rewards the declaration but does not turn it into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"This is an elaborate, controlled response to Lear’s formal request, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Its heightened rhetoric intensifies the avowal but does not independently establish expressive-release force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\\n\\nGONERIL.\\nSir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.85,\"reason\":\"The occurrence has a strategic and competitive setting—it answers Lear’s test and helps Goneril claim his bounty—but that purpose does not establish a distinct core force beyond the truth-presenting avowal represented by T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public dynastic proceeding, Lear announces that he will divide his kingdom and asks his daughters to say which loves him most, linking their answers to his bounty. Called upon first, Goneril addresses Lear with an extended, hyperbolic avowal of unsurpassable filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking by Goneril is established for the focal expression: she claims to love Lear to an extraordinary degree but does not pledge conduct, obedience, care, or continuing loyalty. Lear responds by granting her territory, so he treats the declaration as satisfying his love test; this is uptake of her asserted claim as grounds for reward, not acceptance of a promise. The supplied passage does not settle whether her professed affection is sincere.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"This is the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare’s King Lear. That recognition is disclosed, but the assessment uses only the supplied passage.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supplies the immediate public context and Lear’s response but does not independently establish Goneril’s sincerity, private intentions, or later fulfilment of any filial expectations. No omitted context is assumed to be absent from the work.\"}"
